export abstract anchor FactoryPersistence:
    durability:
        The store supports atomic multi-record commit and durable
        acknowledgement. A crash never loses an acknowledged commit and never
        exposes a partial one. External effects use intent and outcome records:
        one transaction commits intent, request identifier, and reserved version
        before any launch, build, delivery, or worktree effect. Acknowledgement
        covers that commit; the outcome is a second committed operation. An
        in-doubt effect is reconciled by request identifier and effect-class
        discovery, never blindly retried.
    concurrency:
        Each order has an aggregate version. Every mutation presents it; a
        mismatch returns a conflict and applies nothing. The commit set includes
        every record the operation changes and every attempt, result,
        reservation, queue entry, edge, lease, or session reference that must
        change atomically. First-accepted-result selection is an atomic
        compare-and-swap.
        Only one factory process writes at a time, protected by a
        single-instance writer lock covering budget, capacity, admission, and
        all command surfaces. Locks are never held across an external call.
        Multiple readers are allowed. Conflict retries use bounded backoff.
    idempotency:
        Request identifiers are scoped factory-wide. A uniqueness claim is
        inserted before execution so concurrent duplicates cannot both run. The
        record stores actor, target, and a canonical payload fingerprint; a
        reused identifier with different intent is rejected. A duplicate while
        the original is in flight returns in-progress or waits up to the claim
        lease. Stored responses, including rejections and conflicts, replay
        identically.
        A claim has a lease and epoch with adopt, complete, or abandon
        recovery. Idempotency and audit tombstones are retained past target
        record purge so a late retry cannot reapply a destructive action.
    startupValidation:
        Startup acquires the writer lock and validates records, plans,
        surviving sessions, overlap leases, reservations, capacity slots,
        in-flight deliveries, worktrees, idempotency claims, and queue entries
        before accepting operations. Global inconsistencies stop dispatch
        factory-wide in read-only mode and escalate. Order-local failures pause
        only that order. Reconciliation completes before operations resume.
    storeSchema:
        The store has a schemaVersion, migrations, and forward-compatibility
        rules. Status and dashboard read a committed snapshot with a bounded
        staleness defaulting to five seconds and never block writers. Degraded
        mode when the store is unavailable stops new dispatch, keeps running
        attempts, spools bounded outcomes, and marks usage unknown.
